<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-types-of-relations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Types of Relations</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.1 Functions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Identify relations and the different types of mappings
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Guidance on the Opening Activity</title>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Suggested Time</title>
                    <p>
                        10 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Objective</title>
                    <p>
                        To help learners build intuition about how inputs and outputs can be connected before any formal definitions are introduced. The activity moves from familiar everyday relationships to pairs of numbers, so learners are not working in the abstract from the start.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                    <p>
                        Facilitate a short paired discussion. Listen for learners who notice that some relationships allow one person to connect to many others, while other relationships force a strict one-to-one pairing. Avoid confirming or correcting at this stage, the goal is for learners to surface their own observations that you will formalise in the instruction that follows.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

    <!-- Add Activity -->

    <p>
        <term> A relation</term> establishes a connection between elements of a starting set (the inputs) and elements of a destination set (the outputs). In mathematics, the behavior of a relation depends entirely on how these inputs and outputs are mapped to one another.
    </p>
    <p>
        Some relations assign exactly one unique output to each input, while others might map a single input to multiple outputs, or multiple inputs to the same output. Recognizing these mapping patterns is a crucial step in determining whether a specific relation qualifies as a function.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="activity-exploring-relations">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Before looking at pairs of numbers, think about everyday relationships between people. Consider the following real-world scenarios and discuss them with a partner.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Think about the relationship between a child and their father. Can one child have more than one biological father? Can one father have more than one child?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Think about the relationship between a student and the school clubs they belong to. Can one student join more than one club? Can one club have more than one student?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Think about the relationship between a daughter and her mother. Can one daughter have more than one biological mother? Can one mother have more than one daughter?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Think about the relationship between a brother and his siblings. Can one brother have more than one sibling? Can the same sibling be connected to more than one brother in the family?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For each scenario above, describe in your own words how the inputs (the first person or group) connect to the outputs (the second person or group). Try to group the scenarios based on similarities in how the connections work.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now look at the following mappings written as pairs of numbers. Each pair is written as (input, output). Using the patterns you noticed in the real-world scenarios, describe how the inputs and outputs are connected in each case, and decide which of the mappings you think could represent a function and which could not.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(i)">
                        <li>
                            <p>(1, 2), (2, 4), (3, 6)</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>(1, 5), (2, 5), (3, 5)</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>(1, 2), (1, 4), (1, 6)</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>(1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 3), (2, 4)</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on structured lesson plan-->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Suggested Time</title>
                    <p>
                        15 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Objective</title>
                    <p>
                        To formalise the concepts and properties learners discovered in the opening activity by giving precise names and definitions to each mapping type.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways and Teacher Connection</title>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Connecting to Learner Work</alert> — Draw an explicit link between the real-world scenarios in the activity and the formal terms. For example, the child-to-father relationship (one child, one father) connects directly to the idea of a one-to-one mapping, while the mother-to-children relationship (one mother, many children) connects to one-to-many. Naming the types using the learners' own examples will help the definitions feel grounded rather than abstract.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Function or Not</alert> — Emphasise that the key question is always whether each input maps to exactly one output. One-to-one and many-to-one mappings both satisfy this condition, which is why they are functions. One-to-many and many-to-many mappings do not, because at least one input connects to more than one output.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Common Confusion</alert> — Learners often accept many-to-one as a function without difficulty but struggle to explain why one-to-many fails. Spend extra time on this distinction, and return to the library borrower example (one borrower checking out multiple books) to make it concrete before moving to the formal definition.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

    <insight>
        <paragraphs xml:id="para-relation-concepts">
            <title>Types of Relations</title>
            <p>
                Each mapping type is defined by whether a single input connects to one or more outputs, and whether multiple inputs share the same output. The table below summarises the four types, together with a real-world example and whether each type qualifies as a function.
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-relation-types">
                <title>Summary of Relation Types</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell>Type</cell>
                        <cell>What it means</cell>
                        <cell>Real-world example</cell>
                        <cell>Function?</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><term>One-to-One</term></cell>
                        <cell>Each input maps to exactly one unique output; no output is shared</cell>
                        <cell>A child and their biological father — one child has exactly one father</cell>
                        <cell>Yes</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><term>Many-to-One</term></cell>
                        <cell>Several inputs map to the same single output</cell>
                        <cell>Several children sharing the same biological mother</cell>
                        <cell>Yes</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><term>One-to-Many</term></cell>
                        <cell>One input maps to more than one output</cell>
                        <cell>A library borrower who checks out multiple books at once</cell>
                        <cell>No</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><term>Many-to-Many</term></cell>
                        <cell>Multiple inputs each map to multiple outputs</cell>
                        <cell>Students and school subjects — one student takes many subjects, and each subject has many students</cell>
                        <cell>No</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                One-to-one and many-to-one relations are both functions because every input still points to exactly one output. One-to-many and many-to-many relations are <alert>not</alert> functions because at least one input connects to more than one output.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </insight>

    <paragraphs xml:id="para-line-tests-concepts">
        <title>The Line Tests</title>
        <p>
            When a relation is graphed on a coordinate plane, we can quickly determine its properties using visual tests.
        </p>
        <definition xml:id="def-vertical-line-test">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The <term>Vertical Line Test</term> states that a graph represents a function if and only if no vertical line intersects the graph at more than one point. If any vertical line cuts the graph multiple times, it means one input (<m>x</m>-value) maps to multiple outputs (<m>y</m>-values).
                </p>
                <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="0% 10%">
                    <figure xml:id="fig-vlt-pass">
                        <caption>Passes the Vertical Line Test — this is a function.</caption>
                        <image xml:id="img-vlt-pass">
                            <latex-image>
                                \begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, scale=0.75]
                                    \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-2.5,-2.5) grid (2.5,2.5);
                                    \draw[&lt;->, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                    \draw[&lt;->, thick] (0,-2.5) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$y$};
                                    \draw[ultra thick, blue, domain=-1.5:1.5, samples=50] plot (\x, {\x*\x});
                                    \draw[thick, red, dashed, &lt;->] (0.8, -2.5) -- (0.8, 2.5);
                                    \filldraw[red] (0.8, 0.64) circle (3pt);
                                    \node[red, right] at (0.8, 0.64) {\small 1 point};
                                \end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                    <figure xml:id="fig-vlt-fail">
                        <caption>Fails the Vertical Line Test — this is not a function.</caption>
                        <image xml:id="img-vlt-fail">
                            <latex-image>
                                \begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, scale=0.75]
                                    \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-2.5,-2.5) grid (2.5,2.5);
                                    \draw[&lt;->, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                    \draw[&lt;->, thick] (0,-2.5) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$y$};
                                    \draw[ultra thick, blue] (0,0) circle (2cm);
                                    \draw[thick, red, dashed, &lt;->] (1, -2.5) -- (1, 2.5);
                                    \filldraw[red] (1, 1.732) circle (3pt);
                                    \filldraw[red] (1, -1.732) circle (3pt);
                                    \node[red, right] at (1, 1.732) {\small 2 points};
                                \end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                </sidebyside>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <definition xml:id="def-horizontal-line-test">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The <term>Horizontal Line Test</term> is used on a known function to check for uniqueness. A function is one-to-one if and only if no horizontal line intersects its graph at more than one point. If a horizontal line crosses multiple times, it means different inputs share the same output, making it many-to-one.
                </p>
                <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="0% 10%">
                    <figure xml:id="fig-hlt-pass">
                        <caption>Passes the Horizontal Line Test — this function is one-to-one.</caption>
                        <image xml:id="img-hlt-pass">
                            <latex-image>
                                \begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, scale=0.75]
                                    \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-2.5,-2.5) grid (2.5,2.5);
                                    \draw[&lt;->, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                    \draw[&lt;->, thick] (0,-2.5) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$y$};
                                    \draw[ultra thick, blue, domain=-1.4:1.4, samples=50] plot (\x, {\x*\x*\x});
                                    \draw[thick, red, dashed, &lt;->] (-2.5, 0.8) -- (2.5, 0.8);
                                    \filldraw[red] (0.928, 0.8) circle (3pt);
                                    \node[red, above right] at (0.928, 0.8) {\small 1 point};
                                \end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                    <figure xml:id="fig-hlt-fail">
                        <caption>Fails the Horizontal Line Test — this function is many-to-one.</caption>
                        <image xml:id="img-hlt-fail">
                            <latex-image>
                                \begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, scale=0.75]
                                    \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-2.5,-0.5) grid (2.5,2.5);
                                    \draw[&lt;->, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                    \draw[&lt;->, thick] (0,-0.5) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$y$};
                                    \draw[ultra thick, blue, domain=-1.55:1.55, samples=50] plot (\x, {\x*\x});
                                    \draw[thick, red, dashed, &lt;->] (-2.5, 1.5) -- (2.5, 1.5);
                                    \filldraw[red] (-1.225, 1.5) circle (3pt);
                                    \filldraw[red] (1.225, 1.5) circle (3pt);
                                    \node[red, above] at (0, 1.5) {\small 2 points};
                                \end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                </sidebyside>
            </statement>
        </definition>
    </paragraphs>
    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->
   <example xml:id="ex-identifying-relations">
        <statement>
            <p>Determine the type of relation for each of the following mappings, and state whether the relation is a function.</p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="a">
                <li><p>(1, 2), (2, 4), (3, 6), (4, 8)</p></li>
                <li><p>(1, 5), (2, 5), (3, 5)</p></li>
                <li><p>(2, 1), (2, 3), (2, 5)</p></li>
                <li><p>(1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 3), (2, 4)</p></li>
            </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
            <ol marker="a">
                <li>
                    <p>Each input is paired with one unique output, and no output value is repeated. This is a <term>one-to-one relation</term> and it is therefore a function.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>The different inputs (1, 2, and 3) are all mapped to the same output value of 5. This is a <term>many-to-one relation</term> and it is therefore a function.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>A single input value (2) is connected to three different output values. This is a <term>one-to-many relation</term> and it is <alert>not</alert> a function.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>The input 1 is paired with multiple outputs, and the output 3 is mapped from multiple inputs. This is a <term>many-to-many relation</term> and it is <alert>not</alert> a function.</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-vertical-line-test">
        <statement>
            <p>Determine whether the graph of the circle defined by <m>x^2 + y^2 = 9</m> represents a function.</p>
            
            <figure xml:id="fig-vlt-circle">
                <caption>The Vertical Line Test on a circle.</caption>
                <image width="50%" xml:id="img-vlt-circle">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth]
                            \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-4,-4) grid (4,4);
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (-4,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (0,-4) -- (0,4) node[above] {$y$};
                            \draw[ultra thick, blue] (0,0) circle (3cm);
                            \draw[thick, red, dashed, &lt;-&gt;] (1.5, -4) -- (1.5, 4) node[above right] {$x=1.5$};
                            \filldraw[red] (1.5, 2.598) circle (3pt) node[right] {Intersection 1};
                            \filldraw[red] (1.5, -2.598) circle (3pt) node[right] {Intersection 2};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>As shown in the graph, if you draw a vertical line such as <m>x = 1.5</m>, it intersects the circle at two distinct points. Because the graph fails the vertical line test, the relation is <alert>not</alert> a function since one input maps to multiple outputs.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-horizontal-line-test">
        <statement>
            <p>Determine whether the function <m>f(x) = x^2</m> is one-to-one.</p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-hlt-parabola">
                <caption>The Horizontal Line Test on a parabola.</caption>
                <image width="50%" xml:id="img-hlt-parabola">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth]
                            \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-4,-1) grid (4,5);
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (-4,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (0,-1) -- (0,5) node[above] {$y$};
                            \draw[ultra thick, blue, domain=-2.2:2.2, samples=50] plot (\x, {\x*\x});
                            \draw[thick, red, dashed, &lt;-&gt;] (-4, 4) -- (4, 4) node[below left] {$y=4$};
                            \filldraw[red] (-2, 4) circle (3pt) node[above left] {Intersection 1};
                            \filldraw[red] (2, 4) circle (3pt) node[above right] {Intersection 2};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                While the graph of <m>f(x) = x^2</m> passes the vertical line test (meaning it is a function), a horizontal line such as <m>y = 4</m> intersects the parabola at two distinct points: <m>(-2, 4)</m> and <m>(2, 4)</m>. Because the graph fails the horizontal line test, the function is <alert>not</alert> one-to-one and is instead classified as many-to-one.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- If STACK questions are included, uncomment this: -->

    <!-- <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" /> -->

    <!-- Add STACK exercises if made -->

    <!-- Add static exercises -->

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-types-of-relations">
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>State whether the relation below is one-to-one, many-to-one, one-to-many, or many-to-many.</p>
                <p>
                    (1, 2), (2, 4), (3, 6)
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>One-to-One</p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Classify the following relation.</p>
                <p>
                    (1, 5), (2, 5), (3, 5)
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>Many-to-One</p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Determine the type of relation shown below.</p>
                <p>
                    (1, 2), (1, 4), (1, 6)
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>One-to-Many</p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>State the type of relation represented by the following mapping.</p>
                <p>
                    (1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 3), (2, 4)
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>Many-to-Many</p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Which of the following relations are functions?</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="a">
                    <li><p>(1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 4)</p></li>
                    <li><p>(1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 4)</p></li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="a">
                    <li><p>Function</p></li>
                    <li><p>Not a function</p></li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Explain why a circle fails the vertical line test.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>Most vertical lines drawn through the domain of the circle will intersect the graph at exactly two points, meaning a single input (<m>x</m>-value) maps to two different outputs (<m>y</m>-values), so the circle does not represent a function.</p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>Determine whether the function <m>f(x) = x^3</m> passes the horizontal line test.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>Yes. Every horizontal line intersects the graph of <m>y = x^3</m> exactly once, which confirms that the function is one-to-one.</p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

    </exercises>

    <!-- [LP] Add information on how to differentiate  -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table>
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Provide a reference card that shows a diagram of each mapping type alongside its real-world example (for instance, child-to-father for one-to-one and student-to-courses for many-to-many). Encourage these learners to check the diagram before deciding whether a mapping is a function, rather than trying to recall the definition from memory. Reduce the number of exercise questions and focus on parts (a) and (b) of each task before introducing the more complex mappings.
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        The core lesson activities as described above.
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Ask these learners to sketch their own graphs for each type of mapping and apply both the vertical and horizontal line tests to verify their classification. They can also be asked to come up with additional real-world examples of each mapping type that were not used in the lesson, and to explain in writing why their chosen example fits the category.
                    </cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>




       

    

